{"ClientSettings":{"Pn":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"P"},"Sc":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"H"},"Qz":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"T"},"Ap":true,"Mute":true,"Lad":"2026-07-23T00:00:00Z","Iotd":0,"Gwb":0,"Tns":0,"Dft":null,"Mvs":0,"Flt":0,"Imp":1,"Tobn":0},"MediaContents":[{"ImageContent":{"Description":"In France, Port de Saint-Goustan sits just below Auray's upper town, where a small inlet meets the Gulf of Morbihan estuary. The harbour lies at the confluence of the Auray and Loc'h rivers, creating its sheltered, storybook setting. In the 13th century, the Dukes of Brittany built a bridge, a port and a castle here; the fortress has vanished, but cobbled quays, narrow lanes and timber-framed houses still line the water. From Place de la République, Rue du Château passes art studios and galleries down to the waterfront; ramps along the Loc'h, built on old fortifications, open views across the port. Cross the four-arched stone bridge to Place Saint-Sauveur, then climb toward Saint-Gildas Church, where the town rises above the harbour. The quay also marks a transatlantic moment: Benjamin Franklin arrived here in 1776, and a dock now bears his name. Today, cafés and small boats fill the scene, while nearby Auray hosts a well-known weekly market and lively summer events.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.AurayBrittany_ROW4728433531_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.AurayBrittany_ROW4728433531_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Port de Saint-Goustan, Auray, Brittany, France","Copyright":"© Rolf E. Staerk/Shutterstock","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260722_AurayBrittany\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Auray+Saint-Goustan+France&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260722_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260722_AurayBrittany"},"Ssd":"20260722","Name":"AurayBrittany","Market":"ROW","Hash":"4728433531","FullDateString":"22. jul. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Some structures weren't meant to stand out—but time had other ideas. At sunrise, the Santa Catalina Arch glows over the streets of Antigua, Guatemala, its yellow façade catching the light just right as cobblestones stretch ahead and Agua Volcano rises in the distance. The arch was built in the 17th century to solve a practical problem: cloistered nuns needed to cross between convent buildings without being seen from the street. Privacy, not postcards, created one of Central America's most photographed landmarks. Irony holds up well over time.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.SantaCatalina_ROW4470617746_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.SantaCatalina_ROW4470617746_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Santa Catalina Arch, Antigua, Guatemala","Copyright":"© Filippo Maria Bianchi/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260721_SantaCatalina\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Santa+Catalina+Arch+Guatemala&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260721_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260721_SantaCatalina"},"Ssd":"20260721","Name":"SantaCatalina","Market":"ROW","Hash":"4470617746","FullDateString":"21. jul. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"From the moon's cratered surface, Earth can appear as a thin, glowing crescent against the darkness—a rare view that reverses our usual perspective. This stark landscape, shaped by billions of years of impacts, preserves a record of the early solar system. Earlier this year, Artemis II became the first crewed mission to fly around the moon since 1972. Its successful 10-day journey tested the Orion spacecraft and other systems that will support future lunar missions.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.Artemis_ROW4185656902_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.Artemis_ROW4185656902_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Moon and Earth captured by the Artemis II crew","Copyright":"© NASA","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260720_Artemis\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Artemis+II&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260720_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260720_Artemis"},"Ssd":"20260720","Name":"Artemis","Market":"ROW","Hash":"4185656902","FullDateString":"20. jul. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Perched on slender reeds, a group of barn swallows gathers in a moment of stillness that contrasts with their usual speed. Known for swift, acrobatic flight and deeply forked tails, these birds are built for life on the wing. They catch insects midair and travel vast distances between continents. Subtle variations in colour and markings suggest that more than one subspecies may be present, reflecting the barn swallow's extensive global range.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.HirundoRustica_ROW4151191676_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.HirundoRustica_ROW4151191676_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Barn swallows of different subspecies resting together","Copyright":"© Oscar Dominguez/Tandem Stills + Motion","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260719_HirundoRustica\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Barn+swallow&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260719_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260719_HirundoRustica"},"Ssd":"20260719","Name":"HirundoRustica","Market":"ROW","Hash":"4151191676","FullDateString":"19. jul. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Reflected in still water, the Rakotzbrücke—commonly known as the Devil's Bridge—forms a near-perfect stone circle in Saxony, Germany. Commissioned in 1860, the narrow basalt structure was carefully engineered so that its arch and reflection align to form a seamless ring when the lake is still. Made from basalt, a volcanic rock, it reflects precise design rather than practical function, its striking geometry intended purely for visual effect. A popular photo subject, it has also appeared in films, including 'The Matrix Resurrections' (2021). Today, the fragile bridge can only be admired from the shore, as crossing is prohibited to preserve it.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.DevilsBridge_ROW3900508213_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.DevilsBridge_ROW3900508213_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Devil's Bridge in Rhododendron Park Kromlau, Saxony, Germany","Copyright":"© Mike Mareen/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260718_DevilsBridge\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Devils+Bridge+Kromlau&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260718_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260718_DevilsBridge"},"Ssd":"20260718","Name":"DevilsBridge","Market":"ROW","Hash":"3900508213","FullDateString":"18. jul. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"On the remote northeastern tip of Crete, Greece, lies a quiet corner far from typical tourist routes. Today's image shows Vai Beach, a broad curve of golden sand bordered by calm, shallow waters of the Aegean Sea. With sunbeds, umbrellas and showers, it's ideal for swimming, relaxing or enjoying the peaceful atmosphere. But what truly sets Vai apart is just beyond the sand: Europe's largest natural palm grove. It consists of more than 5,000 Cretan date palm trees, a species native to Crete and parts of Turkey.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.VaiUmbrellas_ROW3620558002_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.VaiUmbrellas_ROW3620558002_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Blue sunbeds on a sandy beach in Vai, Crete, Greece","Copyright":"© borchee/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260717_VaiUmbrellas\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Crete+Greece&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260717_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260717_VaiUmbrellas"},"Ssd":"20260717","Name":"VaiUmbrellas","Market":"ROW","Hash":"3620558002","FullDateString":"17. jul. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Some places are built for a single purpose; others keep reinventing themselves. Navy Pier in Chicago, Illinois, United States, falls into the second category. The structure stretches more than 915 metres into the lake and was designed as part of Edward H. Bennett's and Daniel Burnham's 1909 Plan of Chicago. Opened in 1916, the pier was built for shipping, recreation and passenger travel on Lake Michigan. It was renamed Navy Pier in honour of World War I naval veterans.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.NavyPier_ROW3320288892_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.NavyPier_ROW3320288892_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Navy Pier, Chicago, Illinois, United States","Copyright":"© Christopher.F Photography/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260716_NavyPier\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Navy+Pier+Chicago&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260716_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260716_NavyPier"},"Ssd":"20260716","Name":"NavyPier","Market":"ROW","Hash":"3320288892","FullDateString":"16. jul. 2026"}],"PreloadMediaContents":[{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Follow this tiger family through Ranthambore National Park in Rajasthan, India, where every step seems effortless and every shadow could hide a striped giant. These are Bengal tigers, built for both stealth and power: an adult can stretch over 3 metres from nose to tail and weigh up to 230 kilograms, yet walk without making a sound. Their distinctive stripes also play a part in a landscape where visibility is a double-edged sword, helping them blend into tall grasses and dappled forests so prey struggle to spot them.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.TigerFamily_ROW4948587422_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.TigerFamily_ROW4948587422_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"A family of Bengal tigers at Ranthambore National Park, Rajasthan, India","Copyright":"© Archna Singh/Shutterstock","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260729_TigerFamily\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Ranthambore+National+Park&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260729_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260729_TigerFamily"},"Ssd":"20260729","Name":"TigerFamily","Market":"ROW","Hash":"4948587422","FullDateString":"29. jul. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Off Anacapa Island, California, USA, the ocean grows its own forests. Within Channel Islands National Park—also known as the 'Galápagos of North America'—giant kelp can stretch beyond 30 metres in length, forming buoyant ribbons lifted by air-filled bladders and anchored below by rootlike holdfasts.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.ChannelKelp_ROW4859123626_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.ChannelKelp_ROW4859123626_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Kelp","Title":"A kelp forest off Anacapa Island, Channel Islands National Park, California, USA","Copyright":"© Ian Shive/Tandem Stills + Motion","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260728_ChannelKelp\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Channel+Islands+National+Park&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260728_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260728_ChannelKelp"},"Ssd":"20260728","Name":"ChannelKelp","Market":"ROW","Hash":"4859123626","FullDateString":"28. jul. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Some places are best introduced with one word: Yoho. It is a Cree expression of wonder, which is exactly the reaction most people have. Established in 1886, Yoho National Park protects part of the Canadian Rockies in British Columbia. Visitors can stop at Takakkaw Falls, where water plunges more than 370 metres, or explore the world-famous Burgess Shale fossil sites. These rocks preserve the remains of marine animals that lived more than 500 million years ago.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.SummerBC_ROW3830675043_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.SummerBC_ROW3830675043_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Emerald Lake, Yoho National Park, British Columbia, Canada","Copyright":"© Olga Matveeva/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260727_SummerBC\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Yoho+National+Park&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260727_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260727_SummerBC"},"Ssd":"20260727","Name":"SummerBC","Market":"ROW","Hash":"3830675043","FullDateString":"27. jul. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"At sunrise in Honduras' Pig Keys—small, low-lying coastal islets tucked within mangrove-rich wetlands—a red mangrove lifts its tangled roots above glassy water. Red mangroves are often the first trees to colonise tropical coastlines, using their distinctive arching roots to anchor in soft, oxygen-poor mud where saltwater and land collide. These roots help trap sediment, stabilise shorelines and create calm waters where other coastal plants can take hold. Their dense networks also reduce erosion, soften the impact of waves and help shield nearby communities from storms. Beneath the surface, the submerged roots provide shelter for young fish, crabs and shrimp, making these wetlands one of the ocean's most productive nurseries.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.RedMangroveSunrise_ROW3783408236_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.RedMangroveSunrise_ROW3783408236_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Sunrise on a red mangrove in the Pig Keys, Honduras","Copyright":"© Mac Stone/Tandem Stills + Motion","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260726_RedMangroveSunrise\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Red+mangrove&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260726_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260726_RedMangroveSunrise"},"Ssd":"20260726","Name":"RedMangroveSunrise","Market":"ROW","Hash":"3783408236","FullDateString":"26. jul. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"In Isla Cristina, Huelva, Spain, the Atlantic shapes a sunlit coastline where salt marshes, beaches and a rich seafaring tradition set the rhythm of daily life. From above, its wide stretches of fine sandy beach and the channels winding through the saltworks reflect the close connection between the landscape and the fishing industry, which has long defined the town's identity.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.IslaCristina_ROW3744341515_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.IslaCristina_ROW3744341515_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Isla Cristina, Huelva, Spain","Copyright":"© MEDITERRANEAN/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260725_IslaCristina\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Isla+Cristina&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260725_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260725_IslaCristina"},"Ssd":"20260725","Name":"IslaCristina","Market":"ROW","Hash":"3744341515","FullDateString":"25. jul. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"The first thing you notice is the colour. A flock of American flamingos stands in the shallow water on Isabela Island, Galápagos Islands, and the water seems to borrow a hint of pink from their feathers. Those feathers were not always that colour. Flamingos hatch grey and gradually turn pink as pigments from algae and small crustaceans accumulate in their bodies.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.GalapagosFlamingos_ROW2731476245_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.GalapagosFlamingos_ROW2731476245_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Flock of American flamingos, Isabela Island, Galápagos Islands, Ecuador","Copyright":"© Tui De Roy/Nature Picture Library","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260724_GalapagosFlamingos\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=American+flamingos&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260724_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260724_GalapagosFlamingos"},"Ssd":"20260724","Name":"GalapagosFlamingos","Market":"ROW","Hash":"2731476245","FullDateString":"24. jul. 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Some flowers quietly fill a garden. Dahlias arrive as if they have been cast in the lead role. Native to Mexico, these members of the daisy family come in more than 57,000 cultivated varieties, thanks in part to their unusual genetics. Dahlias are octoploids, meaning they have eight sets of chromosomes, which helps create blooms that range from tiny buttons to 36 centimetres across.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.PinkDahlia_ROW5018779817_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.PinkDahlia_ROW5018779817_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Pink dahlia flower","Copyright":"© Harald Biebel/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260723_PinkDahlia\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Dahlia+genus+plant&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260723_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260723_PinkDahlia"},"Ssd":"20260723","Name":"PinkDahlia","Market":"ROW","Hash":"5018779817","FullDateString":"23. jul. 2026"}],"LocStrings":{"LOC_HOMEPAGE_VERTICAL_SCROLL_IOTD":"Dagens bilde","LOC_HOMEPAGE_VERTICAL_SCROLL_IOTD_LEARN_MORE":"Finn ut mer","LOC_HOMEPAGE_VERTICAL_SCROLL_DONATE_NOW":"Doner nå","LOC_HOMEPAGE_VERTICAL_SCROLL_FACT":"Hurtigfakta:","LOC_HOMEPAGE_GALLERY_CARD_TITLE":"Nylige hjemmesidebilder","LOC_HOMEPAGE_VERTICAL_SCROLL_GALLERY_IMAGE_ARIALABEL":"Hjemmesidebilde for {0}","LOC_HOMEPAGE_ON_THIS_DAY_TITLE":"På denne dag i historien","LOC_HOMEPAGE_ON_THIS_DAY_IMAGE_ARIALABEL":"På denne dagen i {0} {1}","LOC_HOMEPAGE_SHARE_TOOLTIP":"Del dagens hjemmeside fra Bing","LOC_HOMEPAGE_WALLPAPERDOWNLOAD_TOOLTIP":"Last ned bildet. Dette bildet kan bare brukes som skrivebordsbakgrunn.","LOC_MUSCARD_DOWNLOAD_LINK":"Last ned dette bildet","LOC_HOM